{
  "body_human_readable": "When working with ontologies, several specialized tools and editors are commonly used. Some of the most popular ones include:\n\n1. **Prot\u00e9g\u00e9**: This is one of the most comprehensive and widely used ontology editors. It has extensive support for plug-ins, making it highly customizable and suitable for both research and education. It features an Entities Panel, integration systems like Resourcemap, and various documentation and reasoning tools< [14] >.\n\n2. **TopBraid Composer**: A visual, all-encompassing tool supporting scalable database backends, multiuser support, and integration with various semantic web standards like RDF, RDFS, and OWL. TopBraid Composer allows users to edit a hierarchy of concepts and classes< [2] >.\n\n3. **OntoStudio**: Known for its graphical interface and support for various formats (OWL, RDF, ObjectLogic). Ontologies can be managed and maintained on a central server, supporting multi-user access and collaboration. OntoStudio offers tools for queries (like SQL and SPARQL), external inference engines, and mapping< [1] >.\n\n4. **Fluent Editor**: A free and open-source tool supporting natural language ontology editing, major Semantic Web standards, and SWRL rule modeling and debugging. It allows integration with R language for statistical analysis and visualization< [5] >.\n\n5. **Swoop**: A web-oriented ontology browser/editor for OWL with features like annotation, history, version control, Resource Holder for reusable entities, and various debugging and diagnosis tools. It is recognized for its unique web-browser-like interface< [6] >.\n\n6. **VocBench**: Designed for collaborative development of RDF datasets (ontologies, thesauri, lexicons), requiring minimal installation efforts and offering dedicated facilities for semantic web work< [9] >.\n\n7. **OBO-Edit**: Specializes in ontology editing with a focus on graph-based manipulation, obsolete term handling, and specific support for certain domain ontologies< [6] >.\n\nThese tools often include features like graphical editors, query builders, plug-in architectures, collaborative project support, version control, and integration with reasoning engines to check for logical consistency in the ontologies< [1] >. \n\nA comparative table in your materials highlights their usability, scalability, stability, integration, and documentation features, reflecting the broad diversity in strengths across ontology engineering tools< [2] >.",
